_durability":0.0,"_gate_fcf_durability":null,"_gp_fcf_durability":null,"_score_fcf_durability":null,"_gate_margins":null,"_gp_margins":null,"_score_margins":null,"_gate_fund_growth":null,"_gp_fund_growth":null,"_score_fund_growth":null,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.05446537812850714,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":true,"_score_shrhldr_yield":71.6,"_gate_share_shrink":0.010067693821461887,"_gp_share_shrink":false,"_score_share_shrink":37.4,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.015590332570031916,"_gp_sbc_dilution":true,"_score_sbc_dilution":74.0,"_gate_insider_own":0.26544002,"_gp_insider_own":true,"_score_insider_own":100.0,"_gate_insider_buying":0.4,"_gp_insider_buying":false,"_score_insider_buying":40.0,"pf":[],"description_full":"Princeton Bancorp, Inc. operates as the bank holding company for The Bank of Princeton that provides various banking products and services. The company accepts various deposit products, including checking, savings, attorney trust, and money market accounts, as well as certificates of deposit. It also offers traditional retail banking services, one-to-four-family residential mortgage loans, multi-family and commercial mortgage loans, construction loans, and commercial business loans, as well as consumer loans, including home equity loans and lines of credit. In addition, the company provides debit and credit cards, money orders, direct deposits, automated teller machines, cashier's checks, safe deposit boxes, online banking, wire transfers, night depository, remote deposit capture, bank-by-mail, online, and automated telephone banking services, as well as payroll-related services and merchant credit card processing services through third party. Further, it offers full online statements, online bill payment, account inquiries, mobile banking, transaction histories and details, and account-to-account transfer services. The company was founded in 2007 and is headquartered in Princeton, New Jersey.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Edward J. Dietzler | President, CEO & Director | Age 60 | Compensation: $983,859 (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 240 people. Revenue per employee of $351K ranks in the below the sector median among Financial Services peers. Each employee generates $86K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $5K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 3\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (8/10).","financial_summary":["Princeton Bancorp, Inc. is a small-cap financial services business with a $303M market cap on $84M of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +5% over the past three years, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory. Operating margins are contracting by -6.2pp, the first warning that competitive pressure is eroding the franchise.","Returns on capital run at 9% against a 8% cost of capital, which means the business barely earns its keep economically.","The business throws off $21M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 24 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.15\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 0.0\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and thin 0.4\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Management returns a meaningful 5.4% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, a strong signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insiders hold 27% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 the strongest alignment there is."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Operating margins contracting -6.2pp YoY \u2014 competitive pressure is eroding the franchise","sev":"red","cat":"trend"},{"text":"Price sits 123% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 2 other valuation measures)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":1.2334232430827212},{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (0.3) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Above-average market volatility (VIX 26)","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Late-cycle dynamics increase the probability of a slowdown","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Credit cycles and regulatory capital \u2014 losses accumulate invisibly in good years and appear all at once, and the time to worry is when underwriting standards are loosest.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Fintech disintermediation \u2014 neobanks, embedded finance, and blockchain rails are unbundling the fee streams that traditional banks took for granted.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Interest rate regime shifts \u2014 a rapid move in rates can invert the yield curve, crush bond portfolios, and trigger deposit flight simultaneously.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend growth and reinvestment","Minimal goodwill on the balance sheet \u2014 low impairment risk"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"How Princeton Bancorp Inc Com (BPRN) Affects Rotational Strategy Timing","source":"news.stocktradersdaily.com","link":"https://news.stocktradersdaily.com/news_release/139/How_Princeton_Bancorp_Inc_Com_BPRN_Affects_Rotational_Strategy_Timing_103126065601_1793444161.html","date":"2026-10-31","timestamp":1793444161.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Princeton Bancorp raises quarterly dividend by 14.% to $0.40/share (NASDAQ:BPRN)","source":"seekingalpha.com","link":"https://seekingalpha.com/news/4659830-princeton-bancorp-raises-quarterly-dividend-by-14-to-040share","date":"2026-10-29","timestamp":1793246243.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentim